<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Great Moravia</h1><p class="st-meta">833 CE – 907 CE (approx.) · capital Veligrad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first major Slavic state in central Europe, where Cyril and Methodius created a Slavic liturgy and alphabet that shaped the Orthodox Slavic world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 833 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmír I</a> of the Moravians drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> and united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> north of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, creating a state centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morava-river">Morava</a> valley. Mojmír's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastislav-of-moravia">Rastislav</a>, installed with East Frankish help in 846, soon turned against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/east-francia">East Francia</a> and built a strong independent principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia's</a> power rested on large fortified centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulcice">Mikulčice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stare-mesto-uherske-hradiste-district">Staré Město</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pohansko">Pohansko</a>, with stone churches and rich graves. In 863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastislav-of-moravia">Rastislav</a> invited the Byzantine brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, who created the Glagolitic alphabet and Old Church Slavonic liturgy. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svatopluk-i-of-moravia">Svatopluk</a> I, the realm expanded into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svatopluk-i-of-moravia">Svatopluk</a> died in 894 his sons quarrelled and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> broke away. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> invasions destroyed Great <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Moravia</a> around 906 to 907; the last mention coincides with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pressburg">Battle of Pressburg</a> in 907. Its disciples carried the Slavic liturgy to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, while its lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-bohemia">Duchy of Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/great-moravia.jpg" alt="Great Moravia" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: Tankred; derivative work by Millenium187 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_moravia_svatopluk_datailed.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>833 CE – 907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Veligrad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mojmír I, Rastislav, Svatopluk I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Church Slavonic, Old Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Moravian Empire, Moravia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=870&amp;focus=great-moravia" aria-label="Great Moravia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5097 450 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Great Moravia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5417 706L5199 691M5417 706L5437 760M5417 706L5330 692M5417 706L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0M5437 760h0M5330 692h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=870&amp;focus=great-moravia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
